WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.490
- [Announcer] Stick around
to the end of the video

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:02.940
for an exciting announcement.

00:00:04.612 --> 00:00:05.916
- Can you say hi.

00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:06.934
- This is where I was.

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:11.017
- She's like, yeah, this
is what we're doing.

00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:11.950
- Yeah, sure.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.330
- I definitely look back on that video.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.530
And I'm like, we were babies.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.437
We had no idea what we were talking about.

00:00:18.437 --> 00:00:21.020
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.020
- [Instructor] My baby's
father is still involved

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.460
in our lives.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.593
Three, two, one, go.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.330
I think last time-

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.280
- I was all the way over there.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.750
Everything's changed.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:47.920
I was 14.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.680
I got pregnant and my
boyfriend was 14 as well.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.680
And after the paternity test,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.610
the court introduced visitation.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.760
She was 10 months old.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.200
Sorry, she was 10 months old,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.950
and she finally got to meet her dad

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:03.510
and the visitation kept up for a while,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.520
but recently he's been getting into fights

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.920
and I'd rather do it alone
than with him at this point.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.570
It was really hard before

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.310
and it's done a complete 180.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.800
We were able to get through everything,

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.680
there was a lot of things to get through.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.830
And I had to let go of a lot of pain

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.180
that I was holding towards him
that was directly affecting

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.160
like co-parenting
because with co-parenting

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.040
it's not about what they did to you,

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.830
it's about you have a kid together

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.930
and he stepped up to the
role and he's an awesome dad.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.730
And I am so grateful.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.170
- Yeah, my boyfriend is
still completely involved

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:39.830
in our daughter's life.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.080
And for me, I'm like so
proud of how he has grown

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.370
as a father because he just really

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.970
was not prepared whatsoever.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.680
And it really took like
a lot of like, just time

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.400
and like patience with him and her

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.060
to just like grow that bond

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.060
'cause I know they struggled at first.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.700
Now he's in school, but
it's still awesome to know

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.093
that he's like completely
like capable of watching her.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.920
- [Instructor] Relationships
change after you have a child.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.163
Three, two, one, go.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.520
- Yeah, completely like friendship wise,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.190
It's so hard to maintain

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.750
like friendships that you
had before you were a mom

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.930
and be able to like go out the same

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.150
and hang out with friends the same.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.670
But then I do have friends
who have like stuck with me

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.600
throughout being pregnant
and having a baby.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.590
And those are now her aunties.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.820
- My friendships or relationships,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.830
they either went positive or negative.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.470
Like none stayed.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:44.010
With my husband, our
relationship has gotten stronger.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.150
Since the last video,
honestly we both emotionally

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.290
have matured a lot.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.110
And with my parents and his parents,

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.790
we've gotten closer because
I feel like when we started

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:57.763
our relationship we were kind of like,

00:02:57.763 --> 00:02:59.310
ugh, I don't wanna go see my mom.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.880
But now we're like, all we wanna do

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.440
is be with both our moms.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.490
- I cried immediately for my
mom and how badly I treated her

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:06.977
as a teenager before I had my daughter.

00:03:06.977 --> 00:03:09.140
And I cried for my sister
how I treated them,

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.200
teachers all of the
things that we were given

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.540
and I was entitled to.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.980
And after having a kid, it all changed.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.580
- Yeah, my view on
relationships, just like I said

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.890
in the first video just changed
just what their purpose is.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.270
Especially when I got thrown
into being a young single mom

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.550
and just lost what felt like everybody

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.270
including my family for a period.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.970
But thank God they all came back

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.410
but that was just complete loneliness.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.330
So I look at relationships
as a huge commitment,

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.810
something that two people go,

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.200
okay, I'm ready to pour into you,

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.900
and are you ready to pour into me?

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.870
- [Instructor] I want to have more kids.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.023
Three, two, one, go.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.400
- Okay, I'm surprised everybody said yes.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.900
So in the last video I was,

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.260
I think about a month into being a mom.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.750
Like he was a newborn,

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.429
and then five months later
I got pregnant again.

00:04:05.429 --> 00:04:06.262
- [Audience] Wow.

00:04:06.262 --> 00:04:07.730
- Yeah, she was planned to.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:11.040
We just don't have a lot
of babies in our family

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.520
and I didn't want him to grow up alone.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.200
And right now I'm on somewhat
agree because two is a lot.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.970
It's scary to have more,

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.540
like I don't wanna plan the next one.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.140
- Eventually I do want
kids, not anytime soon

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.740
because I'm still trying to get my degree.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:31.050
And my boyfriend's in
law school at this point.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.240
Even though I'm excited to have kids,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.640
like a part of me is like,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.200
crap, you have to go
through the baby phase again

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.300
and like all that.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.700
But I know it'll be easier
being older and more stable.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.950
- I'm excited for that.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:46.540
Once you get to four, you're
gonna be like, I miss that.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:48.340
When she did not have
an opinion on anything,

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.173
that was a good time.

00:04:49.173 --> 00:04:52.130
So strongly agree, I would like to adopt,

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.420
I would like a small village of children.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.690
I would like all ages just
running around everywhere.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.200
I think kids are so awesome.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.560
And they're so good at teaching you things

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.240
that you didn't even
know you needed to learn.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:06.220
I feel like my whole life

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.053
God's just gonna be sending me kids.

00:05:07.053 --> 00:05:09.000
- What's your like preferred number?

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:09.833
I'm curious.

00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:10.890
Is it like more than four?

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.280
- Seven's my preferred number.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:13.113
- Oh my God.

00:05:13.113 --> 00:05:13.946
- It's a perfect number.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.780
Yeah , we're gonna have a school
bus just filled with kids.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:17.736
That's my goal.

00:05:17.736 --> 00:05:18.569
- I cannot wait to see it.

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:19.402
I love that.

00:05:19.402 --> 00:05:21.550
Now that I've seen what
it's like to live alone

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:25.690
and be a parent, not still
be at my mom's house,

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.690
it's a lot more and I do want more kids

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.350
but once Everly's grown up,

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:34.930
I want my other kids to be grown up

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.810
so I have time to be myself

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:39.410
and kind of separate
myself from being a parent.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:40.410
Still be a parent obviously,

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.630
I'm still gonna talk to
my kids after they're 18.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.070
So if I just keep popping out babies

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:48.300
I feel like I'm never
gonna learn who I truly am

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.400
to the fullest extent.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:51.320
- [Instructor] Having a child

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.300
was a massive financial burden.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.263
Three, two, one, go.

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:04.520
- I feel like my situation's really unique

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.720
because I do YouTube and
that's how I earn my income.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.800
I also do other things, but
YouTube being the main one

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.760
but without her, I would
not have had this channel,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.890
I wouldn't have found
a place where I to try

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:20.530
to inspire other moms,
but also at the same time

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.500
try to prevent teen pregnancy

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.760
because before I got
pregnant, I was going nowhere.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.910
I was depressed, it was bad.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.170
- I feel like I was blessed
with the opportunity

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:35.980
that I was able to plan
it and have some savings

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:37.960
so it wasn't really a burden.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.710
My husband and I learned how to budget,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.620
we learned how to make investments,

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.480
but the behind the scenes
you have all this whispering

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.460
and talking like, oh, teen mom, no money,

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.990
and immediately correlate it, but.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:54.610
- The biggest childcare
expense I have had to pay,

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:56.920
I mean a lot of people
don't realize taking care

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:59.210
of your kids, that is expensive.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:03.570
There's school, there's work
and things that I could do

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.920
and want to do that I don't/can't

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:08.410
because I'm with my daughter all the time.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:09.620
So I have to be very flexible

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:10.840
at what I can actually do with her.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:12.600
I'm so happy to do so,

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.310
but going to court with my child's father

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.480
was the most expensive
thing I've ever gone through

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.320
my entire life and wasn't debt for.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.010
And just, nobody talks about that.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:23.843
If you're gonna have a kid,

00:07:23.843 --> 00:07:28.060
there's weird expenses that will pop up.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:29.540
- Like when my daughter was born,

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.000
there weren't like a lot of
costs because I was so blessed

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.280
to be able to like breastfeed exclusively.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:36.010
And I feel like that's
one of the biggest costs

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:36.960
like when you have a newborn

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.930
is like how expensive formula is.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.870
So I feel like I never
felt the financial burden

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.210
until I was like, I have to go to work

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:45.800
and she has to go to school.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.370
It is a financial burden

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.960
because I need someone to watch her.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.890
I don't have family, my
boyfriend's in school.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:53.890
This is how it is for a lot of moms

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.830
that you get your paycheck,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.020
and like almost a majority of
it goes straight to childcare.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.030
And so it's like, why am I even working

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.530
if it's all going to childcare?

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.240
But at the same time you do need

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.633
that like surplus amount
that you get, yeah.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.720
- [Instructor] I worry
I'm not a good parent.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.880
Three, two, one, go

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:21.940
- Wow.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:22.847
Go, you.

00:08:22.847 --> 00:08:24.130
(laughing)

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:26.140
- I think it comes down to what you define

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:27.580
good parenting as.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.240
And when it comes down to it,

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.820
a good parent is someone
who loves their child

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.030
and will do everything to support them.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:37.180
There are times where I
feel kind of worried that,

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:40.320
oh crap, did I yell too
much a day or something?

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.200
But the end, I love my child

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.720
and I think that's what
defines a good parent.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.560
- [Instructor] Thinking of your parents,

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:49.670
do you try to emulate your parents

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:50.750
as you parent your children?

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:53.010
Or do you run from that idea?

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:55.710
- I personally run from it
just because it was a lot

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.990
of physical disciplining growing up

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:01.020
and it was more mentally draining.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.313
I feel like my parents' priority
was just be good at school.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.890
And here's food and here's a house

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:11.270
and you're perfectly well taken care of

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:12.940
because you have your necessities

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:16.720
but I feel like emotions should
be added in that category

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.620
not just food, water, and shelter.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.710
- My parents were good parents
and they were very loving

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:26.000
but their priorities as
parents was very different.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.710
I think the old way of thinking

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.320
was if your kids listen to
you, then you're a good parent.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.600
If they follow the rules,
then you're a good parent.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:33.920
Successful adults are not ones

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.170
that follow "rules",

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.340
they're people that
think outside of the box,

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.960
that make good decisions 'cause
they're the right decisions

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.830
not because everyone's watching them.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:44.960
The way of thinking back
then was so different

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.700
and they did the best with what they had.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.260
And I very much love them for that

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.820
but I'm trying to do it differently.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.340
- [Instructor] Having a baby
change my life for the better.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.773
Three, two, one, go.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.610
- So before I had my daughter,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:08.100
I was thinking of what my
suicide note was gonna say.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.763
I was not in a good place.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:14.210
She saved my life and not only
got better, but it was saved

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:17.700
because I had a reason to
life and work on myself.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.630
I had a reason to become
a better person and heal.

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:23.930
Since then my life has gotten so amazing

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.870
in ways that I never imagined possible

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:28.951
because of being a parent.

00:10:28.951 --> 00:10:31.350
- It's no longer, like I'm
just doing this for myself.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:33.240
I mean, I can't compare to you guys yet

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:34.690
'cause I don't have my baby yet.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:37.060
But like, I mean it's as
simple as the getting up

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:40.300
and going to my class but I
have a whole new motivation

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.280
to do something as simple as that

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.160
and strive for my best, so.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.380
- Of course I never
planned for her to come

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:50.560
but when I did find out I was pregnant

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.940
and then once I had her,

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:56.390
it was like this insurmountable
amount of joy and love

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.153
that just sudden really fills your heart.

00:10:58.153 --> 00:11:00.500
Being young and having a child,

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.660
like there's challenges
that come with that.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.870
But when you look at the
big picture of things,

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.290
like we're able to love
them and support them

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:10.730
and breeze these wonderful
little human beings, yeah.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:14.880
- I see my life back then and
I was pretty cold with babies

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.420
and like the people around me.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:18.230
I wouldn't really say I love that much.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.840
And now I'm just like grabbing cheeks

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:22.270
and hugging every single person

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.130
I have the opportunity to
hug because life is short.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.250
And you realize that when you have babies

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:30.280
because we were just talking
about how old they've gotten

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:33.700
since the last video
and it breaks my heart

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:36.650
that they're just going
to go up from here.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:39.020
Like there's no pausing time.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:40.657
- You cannot 'cause I know it's annoying.

00:11:40.657 --> 00:11:42.579
- No, no no, it's okay.

00:11:42.579 --> 00:11:43.812
- You wanna walk here?

00:11:43.812 --> 00:11:45.065
- Can you hi?

00:11:45.065 --> 00:11:45.906
- You wanna go [Inaudible].

00:11:45.906 --> 00:11:46.856
- Where's the baby?

00:11:49.124 --> 00:11:49.957
- Cheese.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:52.773
- Okay, I want hugs.

00:11:54.743 --> 00:11:56.793
Bring it in, bring it in, amazing.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:01.563
- [Announcer] Hey good humans.

00:12:01.563 --> 00:12:02.900
Do you love Spectrum?

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:04.160
If so, you're in luck.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:06.950
We're actually making a
game you can play at home

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:08.300
with your friends and family.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:10.580
Or you can answer your
questions like this.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:12.650
Having a college degree is overrated.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.530
I agree, I have one and
it's never helped me

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:16.630
in any way of my life.

00:12:17.755 --> 00:12:20.338
(upbeat music)

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:25.960
- Oh she walks.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.260
- Did they come over here?

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:34.340
- Oh, are you all right?
(baby coughing)

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.540
- Isn't she cute that
she matches her mommy?

